1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. Nc3 Nd4 5. e5 Nxb5 6. Nxb5 Nd5 7. O-O a6 8. c4 Nb4 9. Nc3 d6 10. exd6 Qxd6 11. d4 cxd4 12. Ne4 Qc7 13. Qa4+ Nc6 14. Nxd4 Bd7 15. Nb5 Qb8 16. Nbc3 e6 17. Rd1 f5 18. Rxd7 Kxd7 19. Qd1+ Ke8 20. Ng5 Qe5 21. g3 Rd8 22. Qb3 Nd4 23. Qxb7 Be7 24. Bf4 Qc5 25. Re1 Qc8 26. Qxc8 Rxc8 27. Rd1 Rxc4 28. Rxd4 Rxd4 29. Nxe6 Rb4 {A position to go down in the annals of curiosities. White had very little time left, Black about a quarter of an hour for the remaining moves to move 40. White played} 30. Nxg7+ {and Black replied 30 ... Rxf4?! White now saw 31 gxf4 Rg8 winning the pinned knight and resigned. This ends the game of course, whatever the means employed by the opponent. Such is the immutable case law of FIDE and a number of sorely aggrieved players and officials down the ages have felt it is totally wrong. An illegal move should be impermissible. (Bernard Cafferty)} 0-1

